--- abstract

The JMAP base protocol (RFC8620) provides methods for synchronizing
the current state of data objects between client and server.  This
extension adds the ability to retrieve historical versions of objects,
including objects that have been destroyed, by extending the standard
Foo/get method, to find destroyed objects with Foo/query, and to
restore them with Foo/set.

--- middle

# Introduction

JMAP ({{JMAP-CORE}} — JSON Meta Application Protocol) provides a
standard set of methods for each data type: Foo/get, Foo/changes,
Foo/set, Foo/query, and Foo/queryChanges.  These methods operate
on the current state of objects.  The Foo/changes method tells a
client which objects have changed, but does not provide the
previous values.

In many applications it is useful to access historical versions
of objects.  For example, a user may want to see previous versions
of a contact record ({{JMAP-CONTACTS}}) or recover a deleted
email ({{JMAP-MAIL}}).

This extension adds history support to the standard Foo/get
method.  When the `urn:ietf:params:jmap:object-history` capability
is included in the request's `using` array, Foo/get gains
additional parameters that allow the client to request previous
versions of objects, including objects that have been destroyed,
Foo/query can search the destroyed objects, and Foo/set can
restore them.

## Conventions Used in This Document

{::boilerplate bcp14-tagged}

# Addition to the Capabilities Object

The capabilities object is returned as part of the JMAP Session
object; see {{JMAP-CORE}}, Section 2.

This document defines an additional capability URI.

## urn:ietf:params:jmap:object-history

The capability `urn:ietf:params:jmap:object-history` being present
in the "accountCapabilities" property of an account represents
support for retrieving historical object versions in that account.
Servers that include the capability in one or more
"accountCapabilities" properties MUST also include the property in
the "capabilities" property.

The value of this property in the JMAP session "capabilities"
property MUST be an empty object.

The value of this property in an account's "accountCapabilities"
property is an object that MUST contain the following information
on server capabilities for that account:

* maxHistoryDuration: "UnsignedInt\|null"

    The number of seconds after replacement for which the server
    intends to retain old versions, or null if the server does
    not have a time-based retention policy.  This is a statement
    of server policy, not a guarantee: retention is best effort,
    and the server MAY discard versions sooner (for example, due
    to storage limits).  A version whose `replaced` timestamp is
    more than this many seconds ago has probably been discarded.
    Clients that need to be certain an object remains available
    should not destroy it.

* supportedTypes: "String[]"

    The names of the data types, from the "JMAP Data Types"
    registry {{IANA-JMAP}} or private types, for which the server
    supports the history extensions in this account.  A server
    MAY support history for only a subset of the types it
    supports, and MAY retain history for only some objects of
    a supported type (for example, only for some folders).
    Clients MUST ignore type names they do not recognise.

# Extensions to Foo/get

When this capability is included in the request's `using` array,
the standard Foo/get method ({{JMAP-CORE}}, Section 5.1) accepts
the following additional request arguments:

* includeReplaced: "Boolean" (default: false)

    If true, the server returns previous versions of the
    requested objects in addition to the current version.
    Each previous version appears as a separate entry in the
    `list` array, with the same `id` as the current version.
    Every entry includes an `objectHistory` property (see
    below).  Entries with the same `id` MUST be ordered by
    `objectHistory.version` (ascending).  Entries for
    different objects MAY be interleaved.

* includeDestroyed: "Boolean" (default: false)

    If true, the server also returns objects that have been
    destroyed.  If `includeReplaced` is false, only the final
    version before destruction is returned.  If `includeReplaced`
    is true, all retained historical versions of destroyed
    objects are returned.  For destroyed objects, every entry
    has a non-null `objectHistory.replaced` value, since every
    version has been superseded (the final version was replaced
    by destruction).

    An object counts as destroyed however it was removed: by
    Foo/set, through another protocol (for example an IMAP EXPUNGE
    {{IMAP}} or a POP3 DELE {{POP3}}), or by the server itself, for
    example when it empties a Trash mailbox automatically or destroys
    a Mailbox with `onDestroyRemoveEmails` ({{JMAP-MAIL}},
    Section 2.5).  Likewise, changes made through other protocols can
    create versions just as changes made through JMAP do.

* historyAfter: "UTCDate\|null" (default: null)

    If supplied, only versions with an `objectHistory.replaced`
    value strictly after this date are returned, plus the
    current (live) version which has a null `replaced` value.
    If null, no time-based filter is applied.

* historyBefore: "UTCDate\|null" (default: null)

    If supplied, only versions with an `objectHistory.replaced`
    value strictly before this date are returned.  The current
    (live) version, which has a null `replaced` value, is not
    returned.  If null, no time-based filter is applied.

    A client can use this to page backwards through history: it
    passes the oldest `replaced` value from the previous response
    as `historyBefore` in the next request.  Since `historyBefore`
    applies to every id in the call, a client paging through the
    history of several ids makes a separate Foo/get call for each
    id.  Clients SHOULD pass the `replaced`
    value exactly as received, rather than parsing and
    re-serialising it, since re-serialising at a lower precision
    could cause versions to be skipped.

* historySkip: "UnsignedInt" (default: 0)

    The number of entries to skip for each id, counting from the
    most recent (the highest version number), before any entries
    are returned.  The current version, if it would otherwise be
    returned, counts as one entry.  For example, a client that
    wants only versions older than the 20 most recent passes
    `historySkip: 20`; any id with 20 or fewer matching entries
    is returned in `notFound`.

    A client can also use this to page backwards through history,
    by adding the number of entries returned for an id to
    `historySkip` in the next request.  This is less robust than
    `historyBefore`: if a new version is recorded between
    requests, the window shifts and one entry is returned again.

* historyLimit: "UnsignedInt\|null" (default: null)

    If supplied, the maximum number of entries to return in the
    `list` array for each id, including the current version if
    any.  For each id, the server MUST return the most recent
    entries (those with the highest version numbers) remaining
    after `historySkip` is applied, not the oldest.  If null, the
    server returns all remaining retained versions, subject to
    its own limits.

The server applies `includeReplaced`, `includeDestroyed`,
`historyAfter`, and `historyBefore` to select the matching
versions of each id, then skips `historySkip` entries, then
returns at most `historyLimit` of the remaining entries.

If `includeReplaced` and `includeDestroyed` are both false, the
server ignores `historyAfter`, `historyBefore`, `historySkip`, and
`historyLimit`.

If the `ids` argument is null, the server returns all objects of
the type as per {{JMAP-CORE}}, Section 5.1.  If `includeDestroyed`
is true, this includes every destroyed object for which the server
still retains any version.

The "maxObjectsInGet" limit and the "requestTooLarge" error
({{JMAP-CORE}}, Section 5.1) apply to the number of distinct ids,
not to the number of entries in the `list` array.  Each id can
have up to `historyLimit` entries.

If, after applying `includeReplaced`, `includeDestroyed`,
`historyAfter`, `historyBefore`, and `historySkip`, no versions of a
requested id remain to be returned, that id is included in the
`notFound` array.

## The objectHistory property

When `includeReplaced` or `includeDestroyed` is true, each object
in the `list` array includes an additional property:

* objectHistory: "ObjectHistoryInfo"

    An ObjectHistoryInfo object with the following properties:

    * version: "UnsignedInt"
      A per-object monotonically increasing number that
      provides ordering among the versions of a single object
      within a response.  Version numbers MAY have gaps (e.g.,
      a server may use internal sequence numbers such as modseq
      values).  The current (live) version of an object always
      has the highest version number.  Servers SHOULD return
      consistent version numbers across requests, but clients
      MUST NOT rely on this — version numbers exist primarily
      to provide ordering within a single response.

    * replaced: "UTCDate\|null"
      The date and time at which this version was superseded,
      either by a subsequent update or by destruction.  If null,
      this is the current live version of the object.  For
      destroyed objects, every version has a non-null `replaced`
      value — the final version's `replaced` is the time of
      destruction.  The `replaced` values of different versions
      of the same object MUST be distinct, so that `historyBefore`
      and `historyAfter` partition the history without ambiguity;
      a server that records two versions with the same timestamp
      can collapse them into a single version.  The value MAY
      include fractional seconds ({{JMAP-CORE}}, Section 1.4),
      but servers SHOULD NOT use more than millisecond precision,
      as many client date libraries cannot represent more.

    * hasOlder: "Boolean"
      If true, the server retains at least one older version of
      this object than this one, whether or not that version was
      returned in this response.  A client can check `hasOlder`
      on the oldest entry returned for an id to learn whether
      more history is available, and retrieve it using
      `historyBefore` or `historySkip`.  The server MAY return
      false even if older versions exist (e.g., if it cannot
      efficiently determine whether older versions remain).  If
      the server returns true, the client MUST NOT assume the
      older versions will still be available in a subsequent
      request, as the server may purge history at any time.

    * expires: "UTCDate\|null"
      The time after which the server expects to discard this
      version, or null if it has no planned time.  The current
      (live) version of an object always has a null `expires`.
      Like `maxHistoryDuration`, this is a best-effort statement
      and not a guarantee: the server MAY discard the version
      sooner, for example when storage runs short, and MAY return a
      different value in a later response.  A client can use this
      to tell the user how long a destroyed object can still be
      recovered, but MUST NOT rely on the version remaining
      available until then.

When `includeReplaced` and `includeDestroyed` are both false
(the default), the `objectHistory` property is not included in
the response, and the behaviour is identical to standard Foo/get.

The client MAY include "objectHistory" in the `properties` argument.
It is returned if and only if `includeReplaced` or `includeDestroyed`
is true, regardless of whether it is listed in `properties`.

Which changes produce a version is server dependent: a version is
a snapshot of the object that the server recorded at some point.
The server is not required to create a new version for every
individual change to an object.  For example, rapid successive
updates may be collapsed into a single version, and a server may
choose not to record a version for changes that only affect
server-computed properties.  The server MAY also prune old versions
in any order, so there can be gaps in the version history available
to the client.  Clients MUST NOT assume that the version history is
complete or contiguous.

The values of server-computed properties (for example, the counts
on a Mailbox) in a historical version are also server dependent.
The server SHOULD return the value the property had at the time of
that version if it has that information.

If a historical version contains a reference to a blob (for
example, the `blobId` of an Email), that blob MUST be downloadable
for as long as the server returns that version.  A version that is
not current does not count as a reference to the blob, for garbage
collection ({{JMAP-CORE}}, Section 6) or for any other purpose, such
as the "blobHasReference" error and Blob/lookup method of
{{JMAP-BLOBEXT}}.  A server that discards a blob referenced by a
version MUST NOT return that version.

If `includeReplaced` or `includeDestroyed` is true and the data type
is not listed in the `supportedTypes` capability property, the
server MUST reject the call with an "invalidArguments" error.

If the data type is supported but the server does not retain
history for a particular object, it MUST still return the current
version with an `objectHistory` property when requested.  In this
case, the server returns only the current version, with a `replaced`
of null.

## Examples

### Contact history

Requesting the history of a contact, including all versions:

~~~json
[["ContactCard/get", {
  "accountId": "abc",
  "ids": ["contact1"],
  "properties": ["name", "emails"],
  "includeReplaced": true,
  "historyLimit": 10
}, "0"]]
~~~

The response includes three versions: the original creation,
a name update, and the current version with an additional email
address:

~~~json
[["ContactCard/get", {
  "accountId": "abc",
  "state": "abc123",
  "list": [
    {
      "id": "contact1",
      "name": {"full": "Robert Smith"},
      "emails": {
        "e1": {"address": "bob@example.com"}
      },
      "objectHistory": {
        "version": 1,
        "replaced": "2026-02-20T14:15:00Z",
        "hasOlder": false,
        "expires": "2026-03-22T14:15:00Z"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "contact1",
      "name": {"full": "Bob Smith"},
      "emails": {
        "e1": {"address": "bob@example.com"}
      },
      "objectHistory": {
        "version": 2,
        "replaced": "2026-03-01T09:00:00Z",
        "hasOlder": true,
        "expires": "2026-03-31T09:00:00Z"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "contact1",
      "name": {"full": "Bob Smith"},
      "emails": {
        "e1": {"address": "bob@example.com"},
        "e2": {"address": "bob@personal.example"}
      },
      "objectHistory": {
        "version": 3,
        "replaced": null,
        "hasOlder": true,
        "expires": null
      }
    }
  ],
  "notFound": []
}, "0"]]
~~~

### Recovering destroyed emails

A client can use Email/changes to discover which emails have been
destroyed, then use a backreference to fetch their final state.
This is useful for displaying a "recently deleted" view:

~~~json
[
  ["Email/changes", {
    "accountId": "abc",
    "sinceState": "state42"
  }, "0"],
  ["Email/get", {
    "accountId": "abc",
    "#ids": {
      "resultOf": "0",
      "name": "Email/changes",
      "path": "/destroyed"
    },
    "properties": ["subject", "from", "receivedAt"],
    "includeDestroyed": true
  }, "1"]
]
~~~

The Email/get response returns the final state of each
destroyed email.  The `objectHistory.replaced` value is the
time of destruction:

~~~json
[["Email/get", {
  "accountId": "abc",
  "state": "state43",
  "list": [
    {
      "id": "email42",
      "subject": "Meeting notes",
      "from": [{"name": "Alice",
        "email": "alice@example.com"}],
      "receivedAt": "2026-03-10T09:00:00Z",
      "objectHistory": {
        "version": 1,
        "replaced": "2026-03-15T16:30:00Z",
        "hasOlder": false,
        "expires": "2026-04-14T16:30:00Z"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "email57",
      "subject": "Project update",
      "from": [{"name": "Bob",
        "email": "bob@example.com"}],
      "receivedAt": "2026-03-12T14:00:00Z",
      "objectHistory": {
        "version": 1,
        "replaced": "2026-03-15T16:31:00Z",
        "hasOlder": false,
        "expires": "2026-04-14T16:31:00Z"
      }
    }
  ],
  "notFound": []
}, "1"]]
~~~

Since `includeReplaced` was not set, only the final version of
each destroyed email is returned.  The non-null `replaced`
value indicates when each email was destroyed.  The server in
this example keeps history for 30 days.

# Extensions to Foo/query {#query}

The Foo/changes approach in {{recovering-destroyed-emails}} only
works for a client that holds a state string from before the
objects were destroyed, and only while the server can still
calculate changes from that state.  Foo/get with a null `ids`
argument and `includeDestroyed` returns every retained object of
the type at once, with no filtering, sorting or paging.

When this capability is included in the request's `using` array,
the standard Foo/query method ({{JMAP-CORE}}, Section 5.5) accepts
the following additional request arguments:

* onlyDestroyed: "Boolean" (default: false)

    If true, the query runs over the destroyed objects of this
    type for which the server still retains at least one version,
    instead of over current objects.  An object that has been
    restored ({{restoring}}) is a current object again, and is not
    included.

* destroyedAfter: "UTCDate\|null" (default: null)

    If supplied, only objects destroyed strictly after this date
    are included.

* destroyedBefore: "UTCDate\|null" (default: null)

    If supplied, only objects destroyed strictly before this date
    are included.

The time an object was destroyed is the `replaced` value of its
final version.

The server MUST reject the call with an "invalidArguments" error if
`onlyDestroyed` is true and the type is not listed in
`supportedTypes`, or if `destroyedAfter` or `destroyedBefore` is
supplied and `onlyDestroyed` is false.

When `onlyDestroyed` is true:

* The `filter` and `sort` arguments apply to the final version of
  each destroyed object.  The server need not support every filter
  condition or comparator for destroyed objects that it supports
  for current objects.  It rejects one it does not support with
  "unsupportedFilter" or "unsupportedSort", as usual.

* If `sort` is null or empty, the results are ordered by time of
  destruction, most recent first.

* Arguments that apply only to current objects, such as
  `collapseThreads` for Email, are ignored.

* Foo/queryChanges works as for any other query.  An object leaves
  the results when it is restored or discarded, and enters them
  when it is destroyed.  A server that cannot track these changes
  MAY return a "cannotCalculateChanges" error.

The ids returned can then be passed to Foo/get with
`includeDestroyed` set, usually with a backreference.

## Example

A client builds a "recently deleted" view of the 50 emails
destroyed most recently since 1 March:

~~~json
[
  ["Email/query", {
    "accountId": "abc",
    "onlyDestroyed": true,
    "destroyedAfter": "2026-03-01T00:00:00Z",
    "limit": 50
  }, "0"],
  ["Email/get", {
    "accountId": "abc",
    "#ids": {
      "resultOf": "0",
      "name": "Email/query",
      "path": "/ids"
    },
    "properties": ["subject", "from", "receivedAt"],
    "includeDestroyed": true
  }, "1"]
]
~~~

The Email/query response lists the destroyed emails, most recently
destroyed first:

~~~json
[["Email/query", {
  "accountId": "abc",
  "queryState": "dq7",
  "canCalculateChanges": true,
  "position": 0,
  "ids": ["email57", "email42"]
}, "0"]]
~~~

The Email/get response is the same as in
{{recovering-destroyed-emails}}.

# Restoring Objects {#restoring}

To restore an earlier version of an existing object, the client
uses a normal Foo/set update with the values from that version.

To restore a destroyed object, the client uses the `restore`
argument of Foo/set.

## Extensions to Foo/set

When this capability is included in the request's `using` array,
the standard Foo/set method ({{JMAP-CORE}}, Section 5.3) accepts
the following additional request argument:

* restore: "Id[PatchObject\|null]\|null" (default: null)

    A map of the ids of destroyed objects to restore.  Each value
    is null, or a PatchObject to apply to the restored object.  An
    empty PatchObject means the same as null.

The response has the following additional properties:

* restored: "Id[Foo\|null]\|null"

    A map of the ids of objects that were successfully restored.
    The value for each is an object containing any properties of the
    restored object whose values differ from those in the final
    version with the patch applied, or null if there are none.  This
    is null if no objects were restored.

* notRestored: "Id\[SetError\]\|null"

    A map of the ids of objects that could not be restored to a
    SetError object explaining why.  This is null if every restore
    succeeded or none was requested.

The server MUST reject the call with an "invalidArguments" error if
`restore` is non-null and the type is not listed in
`supportedTypes`.

The server processes each restore before any create, update, or
destroy in the same call, so these can refer to a restored object.
The restored object takes the id of the destroyed object, and the
values of the final version of that object
({{the-objecthistory-property}}), with the PatchObject applied.  The
patch follows the same rules as a PatchObject in an update in
Foo/set.  This includes server-set properties, such as the
`threadId` and `size` of an Email.  These keep their values from
the final version, unless the server cannot keep them, for
example if the Thread no longer exists; the server then sets new
values and returns them in `restored`.

The history of the restored object continues from that of the
destroyed object, so the destroyed versions become previous versions
of the restored object.  To restore a version other than the final
one, the client includes the values of that version in the
PatchObject.

The server rejects a restore with a SetError as follows:

* notFound: The id is not the id of a destroyed object of this type
  for which the server retains any history.  This includes the id of
  a current object.

* invalidPatch: The PatchObject is not valid.

* invalidProperties: The restored object would not be valid, as for
  an update in Foo/set.

* forbidden: The server does not allow this object to be restored,
  for example because of its content ({{harmful-content}}).

* overQuota: The restored object would exceed a quota.

The server MAY also return any other SetError that Foo/set returns
for an update or create of this type.

The server MUST NOT reuse the id of a destroyed object for which it
retains any history, other than by restoring that object.

Foo/changes reports a restored id in `updated` if the object existed
at `sinceState`, and in `created` otherwise.  It never reports a
restored id in `destroyed`.

## Type-Specific Notes

A restored Email is the original message: it has the same `blobId`,
and so the same content, as the final version.  A client can
restore an Email with Email/set without fetching its content first.

A SieveScript {{JMAP-SIEVE}} is deactivated before it is destroyed
({{JMAP-SIEVE}}, Section 2.4), so a restored SieveScript is not
active.  The client can activate it with `onSuccessActivateScript`,
using its id.

The final version may refer to an object that no longer exists,
such as a Mailbox in an Email's `mailboxIds`.  The server then
rejects the restore as it would an update that refers to a
nonexistent object.  The client can restore that object first, in
the same call or an earlier one, since Mailbox, AddressBook, and
Calendar can have history too.  Or it can choose another one in the
PatchObject, for example the Mailbox with the "inbox" role.  The
server can also reject a restore that conflicts with a current
object, using the error that Foo/set would normally return; for
example, a SieveScript whose name is now used by another script is
rejected with "alreadyExists" ({{JMAP-SIEVE}}, Section 2.4).

## Example

Having found email42 with Email/query (see {{query}}), the client
restores it.  The Mailbox it was in has since been destroyed, so
the client puts it in the Inbox (mb1):

~~~json
[["Email/set", {
  "accountId": "abc",
  "restore": {
    "email42": {
      "mailboxIds": {"mb1": true}
    }
  }
}, "0"]]
~~~

The server restores the Email.  It has kept its `threadId`, so no
properties are returned:

~~~json
[["Email/set", {
  "accountId": "abc",
  "oldState": "s110",
  "newState": "s111",
  "created": null,
  "updated": null,
  "destroyed": null,
  "notCreated": null,
  "notUpdated": null,
  "notDestroyed": null,
  "restored": {"email42": null},
  "notRestored": null
}, "0"]]
~~~

# Design Rationale

This section is not normative.

History is returned through the existing Foo/get method rather than
a new method.  The entries in the `list` array are ordinary objects
of the data type, so a client can feed them to the same parser it
uses for current objects, and the existing `properties` argument
works unchanged.  It also allows a client to fetch the final state
of many destroyed objects in a single call, using a backreference
to the `destroyed` list from Foo/changes, as shown in the examples
above.

The history metadata is embedded in each object as the
`objectHistory` property, rather than wrapping each object in a
separate container, for the same reason: the entries remain
ordinary objects.  This follows the existing pattern of JMAP-specific
properties such as `id` being added to objects whose format is
defined elsewhere; the `objectHistory` name is reserved in the
relevant property registries (see {{iana-considerations}}).

Each entry is a full copy of the object rather than a patch
against the previous version.  Patches would save space, but the
client would need every intermediate version to reconstruct any
one of them, which conflicts with the server's freedom to prune
versions, and the final version of a destroyed object would need
to be returned in full anyway.

Every superseded version carries a `replaced` timestamp.  The
server always knows, at least approximately, when it replaced a
snapshot it was keeping, and the timestamp is what a client needs
to present history to a user and to filter it with `historyAfter`.

Destroyed objects are restored with a new `restore` argument to
Foo/set rather than by creating an object with the old id.  A
create in Foo/set cannot set the id or server-set properties, so the
restored object would differ from the original; an Email created
with Email/set is a new message, not the original one.  Restoring
the final version, with a patch, brings back the object exactly,
and the client need not send the whole object back to the server.

Destroyed objects are found with Foo/query rather than a new
method, for the same reasons: the ids it returns go straight into
Foo/get with `includeDestroyed`.  The query runs over destroyed
objects only, rather than over current and destroyed objects
together, because "recently deleted" is normally a separate view.
It also lets a server keep destroyed objects in a separate index.

# IANA Considerations

## JMAP Capability Registration for urn:ietf:params:jmap:object-history

IANA is requested to register the "Object History" Capability
as follows:

Capability Name: urn:ietf:params:jmap:object-history

Intended use: common

Change Controller: IETF

Specification document: this document

Security and privacy considerations: this document,
Security Considerations

## Update to the JMAP Data Types Registry

This document adds a "Supports History" column to the "JMAP Data
Types" registry {{IANA-JMAP}}.  The value
is "Yes" or "No", indicating whether the data type supports the
history extensions defined in this document.

The initial values for existing registrations are:

| Type Name                 | Supports History |
|---------------------------|------------------|
| Core                      | No               |
| PushSubscription          | No               |
| Mailbox                   | Yes              |
| Thread                    | No               |
| Email                     | Yes              |
| EmailDelivery             | No               |
| SearchSnippet             | No               |
| Identity                  | Yes              |
| EmailSubmission           | No               |
| VacationResponse          | Yes              |
| MDN                       | No               |
| Quota                     | No               |
| SieveScript               | Yes              |
| Principal                 | No               |
| ShareNotification         | No               |
| AddressBook               | Yes              |
| ContactCard               | Yes              |
| Calendar                  | Yes              |
| CalendarEvent             | Yes              |
| CalendarEventNotification | No               |
| ParticipantIdentity       | Yes              |
| FileNode                  | Yes              |

FileNode is registered by {{JMAP-FILENODE}}.

The default value for this column is "No".  New registrations
that do not explicitly include a value are assumed not to
support history.

This column records whether history is meaningful for a data type.
Whether a particular server supports history for a type is
advertised in the `supportedTypes` capability property.

