# Security Considerations

All security considerations from {{JMAP-CORE}} apply to this
document.

## Storage and Resource Consumption

Retaining historical versions of objects can consume significant
server storage.  Servers SHOULD impose reasonable limits on
history retention, either by duration (advertised via
`maxHistoryDuration`) or by total storage.  Servers MAY
silently discard history entries when storage limits are
reached.

Counting retained history towards a user's quota ({{JMAP-QUOTAS}})
is NOT RECOMMENDED.  If it counts, a user who destroys objects to
free space sees no change until the history expires.  A restored
object counts towards quota as any newly created object does.

## Access Control

Historical versions of an object MUST respect the same access
controls as the current version.  A user who does not have
permission to read an object MUST NOT be able to read its
history.

Access rights can change over time, for example when an object is
shared with a new user, or moved into a shared container.  The
server MUST NOT return a historical version to a user who could not
have read that version at the time it was current.  This requires
the server to track changes to access rights.  A server that does
not keep a full history of access rights can take a conservative
approach: record the point at which the access rights affecting an
object (or its containing object, such as a Mailbox or AddressBook)
last changed, for example as a modseq, and not return any version
that was replaced before that point.

Similarly, for destroyed objects, the server MUST NOT reveal the
existence or content of a destroyed object to a user who could not
read it at the time it was destroyed.

## Information Disclosure

Object history may reveal information that the user or an
administrator intended to remove.  For example, a contact
record that was updated to remove a phone number will still
have that phone number visible in its history.  Servers
SHOULD provide administrators with the ability to purge
history for specific objects when required for privacy or
compliance reasons.

## Privacy Considerations

When a server supports this extension, updating or destroying an
object does not immediately remove the previous content from the
server; it remains retrievable by anyone with read access to the
object until the server discards it under its retention policy.
Users may not expect this.  This is not new in kind (servers
commonly retain deleted data in backups, and many mail systems
offer a "recently deleted" view), but this extension makes such
data retrievable through the protocol.

Servers SHOULD make their retention policy visible to users, for
example through the `maxHistoryDuration` capability property and
the server's user interface.  Clients that present a "destroy"
operation to users SHOULD NOT describe it as irreversible or
permanent when the server advertises this capability for that data
type.  Whether, and how, a user can request that history be purged
before the end of the retention period is a matter of server
policy and is outside the scope of this document.

## Harmful Content

An administrator may remove content from an account because it is
harmful, for example a phishing message or malware.  The server MAY
decline to retain history for such an object, and MAY reject an
attempt to restore it with a "forbidden" SetError, so that the user
cannot bring the content back.

# Changes

EDITOR: please remove this section before publication.

The source of this document exists on github at: https://github.com/brong/draft-gondwana-jmap-object-history/

**draft-ietf-jmap-object-history-03**

* Added John Coffey as co-author; folded in material from
  draft-coffey-jmap-undelete.
* Added Foo/query arguments `onlyDestroyed`, `destroyedAfter`, and
  `destroyedBefore`, for finding destroyed objects without an old
  state string.
* Replaced restoring by creating with the old id (and Email/import
  with the old id) with a new Foo/set `restore` argument, which
  restores the final version with an optional patch, keeping
  server-set properties.  Notes on Email, SieveScript, missing
  containers, and conflicts.
* Added a per-version `expires` to `objectHistory`.
* Destruction through other protocols and by the server counts as
  destruction.
* Security: quota, harmful content.
* Paging several ids with `historyBefore` takes one Foo/get call per
  id.
* The history arguments are ignored unless `includeReplaced` or
  `includeDestroyed` is true.
* Foo/changes reports a restored id as updated or created, never as
  destroyed.
* Versions that are not current do not count as blob references.
* Added FileNode to the "Supports History" table.
* Thanks to Mark Atwood for an implementation review.

**draft-ietf-jmap-object-history-02**

* Replaced the top-level `hasMoreHistory` response property with a
  per-entry `objectHistory.hasOlder` property, so the client can
  tell which ids have more history.
* Added `historySkip`, to skip the N most recent versions of each
  id; ids with N or fewer versions are returned in `notFound`.

**draft-ietf-jmap-object-history-01**

* Removed "Updates: 8620"; the JMAP Data Types registry is not
  defined by RFC 8620.  The registry is now referenced by name
  rather than by its defining document.
* Corrected the registry table to match the current registry.
* `historyLimit` is now per id.
* Defined behaviour for `ids: null` with `includeDestroyed`, and
  clarified that "maxObjectsInGet" counts ids, not entries.
* Ids with no matching versions are returned in `notFound`.
* Which changes create a version, and the values of server-computed
  properties, are server dependent.
* Blobs referenced by returned versions must be downloadable.
* Expanded access control considerations for changing access rights.
* Added `supportedTypes` to the account capability; requesting
  history for an unsupported type is an error.
* Reserved the `objectHistory` property name in the JSContact and
  JSCalendar property registries.
* Added acknowledgements.
* Added a Design Rationale section (Foo/get rather than a new method,
  embedded metadata, full objects rather than patches, timestamps).
* Added a Restoring Objects section: a client may ask to recreate a
  destroyed object with its old id, and the server may accept or
  reject; ids of destroyed objects are otherwise not reused.
* `maxHistoryDuration` is now a best-effort statement of server policy.
* Clarified that "objectHistory" may appear in `properties`.
* Added Privacy Considerations.
* Added `historyBefore` for paging backwards through history;
  versions of the same object have distinct `replaced` values, with
  at most millisecond precision.
* Removed the audit use case from the introduction.

**draft-ietf-jmap-object-history-00**

* Uploaded with working-group adopted name.


**draft-gondwana-jmap-object-history-00**

* initial proposal

